<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Watling Street</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Watling Street is an ancient route in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, paved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as a main road from the Kent ports through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to Wroxeter. Around 886 it formed part of the boundary between the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>. Much of its course is followed by modern roads such as the A2 and the A5.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/watling-street.jpg" alt="Watling Street" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LlywelynII · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1911Watling_Street.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
